#{ ===========================================================================
   EPHEMERALS -- the second nature (0.1): events.

   An ephemeral is defined on the fly and VAPORATES UPON CONSUMPTION. It is
   never had: no construction ceremony, no destruction, no ledger -- it is
   an OCCURRENCE, not a possession. Ephemerals are the language's channel
   of communication, and ONE concept wears two coats (14.1):

     on the BUS:            an event fires, a cause consumes it -- the
                            reaction runs, the event is gone;
     on the FAULT CHANNEL:  a signal leaves a work through exit:, a handler
                            arm consumes it -- the arm runs, the signal is
                            gone. Payloads may travel; tags may not (12.10).

   Consumption IS the ephemeral's end. There is nothing to clean up --
   which is exactly why misbehaviour travels this way (see
   explain-destruction-cannot-fail.vut).

   HONESTY NOTE: the event-typed panel entry 'level : event(...)' (14.2)
   is settled law, not yet built; this file demonstrates both coats over
   implemented surface.
   =========================================================================== }

#{ COAT ONE -- the bus. 'dial_turned' below exists only between its firing
   and the cause that consumes it; the payload rides along and is read as
   e.<field> inside the reaction. After the instant: vaporated. }

meter : class has: { v: float; } {
    from : work
}
+meter.from : work(knows: raw
                   gives: m : meter
                   signals: out_of_range(v)) {
    if: raw > 100 { exit: out_of_range(raw); }
    m = raw;
    give:
}

#{ Demo is active from load (top-level entities auto-activate); posting
   SHOW fans out INSIDE it: the first causality EMITS the ephemeral, the
   second CONSUMES it one instant later -- birth and death on the trace. }

Demo : behavior has: { shown: float; } {
    SHOW => dial_turned(delta = 42);
    dial_turned when: e.delta > 0 => {
        #{ COAT TWO -- the fault channel. The signal 'out_of_range' is an
           ephemeral born at the exit:, consumed by the arm that matches;
           its payload 'v' materialises inside that arm only (12.6). }
        b.shown = meter.from(e.delta) else: {
            out_of_range(v) => { b.shown = 100; }
        }
    }
}
